    {
      "id": "pipeline-effect",
      "title": "Pipeline effect",
      "deck": "SET/GET mix (1:10 ratio) with 100-byte values, comparing pipeline depth 1 vs 30. Candidate statistic: pipeline-30 throughput.",
      "unit": "ops/s",
      "lowerIsBetter": false,
      "verdict": {
        "winnerId": "kividb",
        "headline": "KiviDB 22.4M ops/s at pipeline 30, a floor with the client at 72% CPU; Dragonfly 17.1M; Redis 1.48M; Valkey 1.37M",
        "summary": "SET/GET mix (1:10) with 100-byte values at pipeline depth 30, 200 connections. KiviDB's slowest pass, 20.9M ops/s, beat Dragonfly's fastest, 17.3M, and its median led by 31%. That number is a floor: the client averaged 72% CPU in KiviDB's cell (70 to 73% per pass), where 50 memtier threads can keep at most 50 of its 64 vCPUs busy, and KiviDB's passes ran faster when the client got more CPU (r = 0.91 over ten passes). Dragonfly's cell ran the client at 34%. Pipelining raised throughput 14.9 times for KiviDB, 12.1 for Dragonfly, 8.4 for Redis and 7.7 for Valkey, and Redis led Valkey by 8%."
      },
