    {
      "id": "memory-efficiency",
      "title": "Memory efficiency",
      "deck": "Baseline-corrected memory after loading 1M keys with 100-byte values into a fresh engine. RSS delta is the primary metric (allocator-neutral). Candidate statistic: memtier's default values, 100 ASCII bytes; a second load of random bytes shows what packing them is worth. Two more views of the ASCII cell: the next million keys' own RSS, which no startup allocation absorbs, and the absolute RSS after the first million.",
      "unit": "MB",
      "lowerIsBetter": true,
      "verdict": {
        "winnerId": "dragonfly",
        "headline": "Dragonfly 135 MB RSS delta (142 B/key); KiviDB 142 MB (149 B/key); Redis 158 MB (166 B/key); Valkey 163 MB (171 B/key)",
        "summary": "1,000,000 keys with 100-byte values loaded into a fresh engine; the ranked statistic is the used_memory_rss delta over the empty engine's baseline, on memtier's ASCII values. Dragonfly used 5% less than KiviDB, 15% less than Redis and 17% less than Valkey, and KiviDB used 10% less than Redis and 13% less than Valkey. The delta leaves out what an engine allocates before the first key: Dragonfly's idle RSS was 280 MB at 64 threads, against 12 to 24 MB for the other three, so after the first million its total RSS was 415 MB to KiviDB's 161, Valkey's 175 and Redis's 182. The next million cost Dragonfly 135 bytes per key of RSS and KiviDB 146, and by their own used_memory 147 each. With random bytes, which Dragonfly can't pack 7 bits a byte, its used_memory per key rose from 130 to 146, level with KiviDB's 147."
      },
